<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sannyasa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as sanyasa, sannyasi, sannyasini</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sannyasa is the fourth of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> life stages, the ashramas, after the celibate student, the householder and the forest dweller: a life of renunciation of material desires given to spiritual pursuits. A man in it is a sannyasi and a woman a sannyasini; they typically live simply, detached and itinerant, often in saffron or ochre clothes, alone or with fellow renunciates, seeking moksha, liberation. The term is rare in the earliest Vedic texts, and sannyasa became a recognised stage of life by about the 3rd and 4th century CE. From the 12th century through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a>, some Shaiva and Vaishnava ascetics also formed military orders and fought as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sannyasa.jpg" alt="Sannyasa" width="330" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist. Digitized by Davinder Singh Toor. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photograph_of_a_Sanyasi_ascetic,_albumen_print,_by_Captain_W.W._Hooper_&amp;_Surgeon_G._Western,_Hyderabad,_ca.1865.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It first covered castes such as the Ahirs, Gurjars and Jats, and by the late 19th century its enforcement reached most Shudras and untouchables, <b>Sannyasis</b> and hill tribes.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li><li><blockquote>The poem was published in 1882 in his novel Anandamath, set during the <b>Sannyasi</b> Rebellion of the previous century, and Jadunath Bhattacharya was asked to set a tune for it soon after it was written.</blockquote><a href="/songs/vande-mataram">Vande Mataram</a></li></ul></section>
